s in france there was a  meetup of of people who were interested  in speaking different languages there  must have been a hundred and some odd  people there there were tables  for you know german russian chinese  japanese spanish italian you name it and  all kinds of people were taking turns  going from table to table  trying out their different languages  boy i'd like to do something like that  here in vancouver or elsewhere  i don't know uh  somehow as i often say people are  conditioned to think of language  learning as something that was done in  school that was very unpleasant  and it's a chore and it's a task and  it's very difficult  and i i don't think it need be but maybe  we can create some excitement around the  idea of multilingualism  i met at the office of  the language commissioner or  commissioner for multilingualism in  brussels on my recent trip  tried to get them interested in link of  course by the way i have a linked  t-shirt on this is the front now let's  see if i can just do this so that you  can see i don't know if this if you can  see it but it says i hope i learned  russian at length  anyway a little bit of  publicity but link is only one of of  many tools that are now available uh on  the web for language learning  multilingualism is much more accessible  today than ever before  uh  when we get to the subject of how we  learn languages and you've heard me rant  before i must say that the the book that  i recently read in germany uh in german  called lehnen  here  uh  by manfred spitzer very interesting book  i commented on the book as well at my  blog  and and the big thing that that came out  of that is that the brain  the brain is very capable it learns all  the time it learns all the time it makes  its own rules it doesn't need to have  things explicitly shoved at it  from observation with the right input  it'll pick things up  and we're constantly learning uh and and  i guess the the point that schmitzer  makes is that often schools  